{title=Backward induction for the American put when the risk-free multiplier is one and the up probability is one third. Each node shows the stock price and option value.}

= 5
{parent=B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Put $\sigma=\sqrt{\sigma_1^2+\sigma_2^2}>0$. The linear combination $W_3$ starts at zero and has continuous paths. An increment over an interval of length $h$ is a linear combination of independent centred <normal random variables>, so is centred normal with variance
$$
\left(\frac{\sigma_1^2}{\sigma^2}+\frac{\sigma_2^2}{\sigma^2}\right)h=h.
$$
Increments on disjoint intervals are independent, since they depend on disjoint increments of the two independent <Brownian motions>. These are the defining properties, so **$W_3$ is a standard Brownian motion**.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Cubing the supplied <geometric Brownian motion> solution gives $S_t^3=s^3\exp((3\mu-3\sigma^2/2)t+3\sigma W_3(t))$. The <moment-generating function of a normal distribution> yields $\mathbb E[e^{aW_3(t)}]=e^{a^2t/2}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[S_t^3]=s^3\exp\bigl((3\mu+3\sigma^2)t\bigr).}
$$

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

For this stock-dependent payoff, replace the stock drift by $r$ under a <risk-neutral measure>. Conditional lognormal moments then give, with $\tau=T-t$,
$$
\boxed{V_t=e^{-r\tau}\mathbb E^Q[S_T^3\mid\mathcal F_t]
=S_t^3e^{(2r+3\sigma^2)\tau}.}
$$
This is the <power payoff in the Black-Scholes model>. The <delta hedge> is $\Delta_t=\partial V/\partial S=3V_t/S_t$ shares, so the stock holding is worth $3V_t$. The rest is a bank position worth $-2V_t$, or $-2V_t/B_t$ units of the bank asset. This gives a <replicating portfolio>; its self-financing identity follows from Itô's formula and the Black-Scholes pricing equation, and its terminal value is $S_T^3$.

The full two-Brownian filtration contains an additional orthogonal noise direction, but this particular payoff is replicated using the traded stock and bank asset, so that extra noise does not affect its price.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Apply <Itô's formula> to $V_t=A(t)S_t^3$, where $A'(t)=-(2r+3\sigma^2)A(t)$. Since $dS_t/S_t=\mu\,dt+\sigma\,dW_3(t)$, the drift and diffusion are
$$
\frac{dV_t}{V_t}=\bigl(-(2r+3\sigma^2)+3\mu+3\sigma^2\bigr)dt+3\sigma\,dW_3(t).
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\overline\mu=3\mu-2r,\qquad\overline\sigma=3\sigma.}
$$
Under the risk-neutral measure its drift becomes $3r-2r=r$, as required for a traded, non-dividend-paying replicating asset.

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}
{c}

For a <call option on a power payoff>, the replicating asset $V$ from part (c) has $V_T=S_T^3$, volatility $3\sigma$, and risk-neutral drift $r$. The new payoff is therefore a <European call option> on $V$, with strike $K$. Applying the given <Black-Scholes formula> gives
$$
\boxed{C_t=V_t\Phi(D_1)-Ke^{-r\tau}\Phi(D_2),\qquad
D_1=\frac{\log(V_t/K)+(r+9\sigma^2/2)\tau}{3\sigma\sqrt\tau},\quad
D_2=D_1-3\sigma\sqrt\tau.}
$$
Here $V_t=S_t^3e^{(2r+3\sigma^2)\tau}$. Equivalently $D_1=[\log(S_t^3/K)+(3r+15\sigma^2/2)\tau]/(3\sigma\sqrt\tau)$. It is the price process $V_t$, rather than the raw power $S_t^3$, that serves as the self-financing underlying for this application.

= 6
{parent=B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Under the physical probability, the stock has mean $\mathbb E S_t=50e^{0.1t}$. Nonnegativity permits interchanging expectation and integration in the accumulated dividends. Hence
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E X_T&=50e^{0.1T}+0.01\int_0^T50e^{0.1t}e^{0.05(T-t)}\,dt\\
&=50e^{0.1T}+10(e^{0.1T}-e^{0.05T}).
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E X_T=60e^{0.1T}-10e^{0.05T}\text{ pounds}.}
$$

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Let the accumulated bank account from past dividends be
$$
A_t=\int_0^t0.01S_u e^{0.05(t-u)}\,du.
$$
Holding one stock share and banking its dividends is a <self-financing portfolio> with wealth $X_t=S_t+A_t$ and $dA_t=0.05A_tdt+0.01S_tdt$. Doubling that strategy replicates the specified terminal payoff. Its price is therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{price at }t=2X_t=2S_t+2\int_0^t0.01S_u e^{0.05(t-u)}\,du.}
$$
In particular its initial price is one hundred pounds. This illustrates <banking stock dividends versus reinvesting them>. The accrued-dividend term is known at time $t$ and belongs to the payoff defined from time zero; it cannot be discarded when $t>0$.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

For an ex-dividend stock, holding $\Delta$ shares earns both $\Delta\,dS$ and the dividend gain $\Delta\delta S\,dt$. Freeze $\Delta=V_S$ locally in a self-financing hedge that is long the claim and short these shares. <Itô's formula> cancels its Brownian exposure, leaving drift $V_t+\tfrac12\sigma^2S^2V_{SS}-\delta SV_S$. Its value is $V-SV_S$, so absence of arbitrage requires this drift to equal $r(V-SV_S)$. Rearrangement gives the <Black-Scholes equation with continuous stock dividends>:
$$
V_t+(r-\delta)SV_S+\tfrac12\sigma^2S^2V_{SS}-rV=0.
$$
With the given parameters,
$$
\boxed{V_t+0.04SV_S+0.02S^2V_{SS}-0.05V=0,\qquad V(T,S)=\Psi(S).}
$$
The physical drift $0.1$ cancels because the hedge removes the stock-price risk; the dividend payment remains in the stock's total gain.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

The diffusion associated with the displayed pricing equation is $d\widetilde S_s=0.04\widetilde S_sds+0.2\widetilde S_s dW_s$. For $Y_s=e^{-0.05s}V(s,\widetilde S_s)$, <Itô's formula> gives
$$
dY_s=e^{-0.05s}\bigl(V_t+0.04\widetilde S_sV_S+0.02\widetilde S_s^2V_{SS}-0.05V\bigr)ds
+e^{-0.05s}0.2\widetilde S_sV_S\,dW_s.
$$
The drift vanishes by the PDE. Under the usual integrability conditions for the payoff and solution, localization and conditional expectation make $Y$ a true martingale; for polynomial-growth data the lognormal moment bounds provide the needed integrability. Applying the terminal condition yields the <Feynman-Kac formula>
$$
\boxed{V(t,S)=e^{-0.05(T-t)}\mathbb E[\Psi(\widetilde S_T)\mid\widetilde S_t=S].}
$$
The printed diffusion contains an additional undefined factor $\sigma$ multiplying $0.2\widetilde S_s dW_s$. If literally retained, its second-order generator coefficient would be $0.02\sigma^2S^2$, so the requested representation would correspond to that different PDE unless $\sigma^2=1$. The formula above uses the volatility $0.2$ specified by the stock model and by part (c).

= e
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}
{c}

Under the pricing measure, the ex-dividend stock drift is $r-\delta=0.04$. Thus
$$
\boxed{V(t,S_t)=2e^{-0.05(T-t)}\mathbb E^Q[S_T\mid\mathcal F_t]
=2S_t e^{-0.01(T-t)}.}
$$
A direct replication holds $2e^{-\delta(T-t)}$ shares and reinvests every dividend into more shares. The share count grows at rate $\delta$, reaching two shares at expiry, with no external cash input. This payoff contains only the terminal ex-dividend stock value, unlike part (b), which also includes the separately accumulated dividends.
